infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title State vs behavior verification: which doubles, which assertion
  desc Stubs and fakes let you assert on outcome and survive refactors; spies and mocks pull you toward asserting on interaction and pin the test to the implementation
  compares
    - label State verification — stub + fake
      children
        - label Stub returns canned values
          desc Steers the code down a chosen path; keeps no memory of how it was called
        - label Fake is a real lightweight impl
          desc In-memory Map-backed repo; holds real state and honours the same contract
        - label Assert on the outcome
          desc Check what state the system ended in, not which methods ran
        - label Survives refactors
          desc A behavior-preserving change keeps the suite green
    - label Behavior verification — spy + mock
      children
        - label Spy records how it was called
          desc A stub that also captures arguments and call count
        - label Mock carries expectations
          desc Pre-programmed with the calls it should receive; fails the test itself
        - label Assert on the interaction
          desc Which methods got called, in what order
        - label Brittle — pinned to the impl
          desc A refactor with identical behavior can still turn the suite red
theme
  palette #14b8a6 #f43f5e
